<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mohammad Nadir Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mohammad Nadir Shah was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> from 1929 to 1933. A general from the Musahiban branch of the royal family, he overthrew the Saqqawist ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habibullah-kalakani">Habibullah Kalakani</a> in 1929 and took the throne. He issued a constitution in 1931 and moved cautiously on reform after the turmoil of Amanullah's reign, but was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in November 1933 and succeeded by his son Zahir Shah.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohammad-nadir-shah.jpg" alt="Mohammad Nadir Shah" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nadir_Khan_of_Afghanistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nadir Khan was born in Dehradun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, on 9 April 1883, into the Musahiban branch of the ruling family, of the Mohammadzai section of the Barakzai Pashtuns. His ancestors had been exiled to British India by Emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan">Abdur Rahman Khan</a>, who distrusted their ambitions. Nadir helped the Afghan government defeat the Khost rebellion in 1912, became a general under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah Khan</a>, and led the Royal Afghan Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-afghan-war">Third Anglo-Afghan War</a>. He then served as Minister of War and, from early 1924 to 1926, as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Disagreements with Amanullah led to Nadir's exile shortly after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habibullah-kalakani">Habibullah Kalakani</a>'s rebellion against the monarchy. When Kalakani overthrew Amanullah, Nadir returned with an army of Mangal, Mahsud and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">Wazir</a> tribesmen, with British support, and took most of the country. His forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> by 13 October 1929 and sacked it, and he arrived on the 15th. Kalakani was shot on 1 November 1929 with his brother and nine of his circle.</p><p>As king, Nadir quickly abolished most of Amanullah's reforms. The army remained weak while religious and tribal leaders grew strong, and he faced many revolts, among them the Koh Daman revolt, the Shinwari rebellion of February 1930 and the Darre Khel revolt of November 1932. Most had been subdued by the end of 1931. A loya jirga of 286 members confirmed his accession in September 1930, and in 1931 he promulgated a constitution that, despite its constitutional appearance, in effect set up a royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nadir modernised more quietly than Amanullah. He improved roads, notably the Great North Road through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>, and helped establish Kabul University in 1931. He forged commercial links with foreign powers, began a banking system and long-range economic planning, and by 1933 had built a military force of 40,000.</p><p>On 8 November 1933 he was shot dead by Abdul Khaliq Hazara at a school graduation ceremony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. The assassin was executed along with most of his relatives, and the government used the occasion to arrest other rivals. Nadir was buried on Maranjan Hill, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal">Muhammad Iqbal</a> wrote an elegy for him. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-zahir-shah">Mohammad Zahir Shah</a> succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mohammad Nadir Shah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Amanullah's reforms provoked a revolt that drove him out in 1929, and after the brief Saqqawist Emirate, <b>Mohammad Nadir Shah</b> took the throne.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-afghanistan">Kingdom of Afghanistan</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mohammad Nadir Khan</b>, a former general, raised Pashtun tribes and took Kabul in October 1929.</blockquote><a href="/state/saqqawist-emirate">Saqqawist Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dehradun, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.32,78.03&amp;year=1915" aria-label="Dehradun on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6574 1057 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mohammad Nadir Shah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7024 1327h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7024 1327h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.32,78.03&amp;year=1915">What was here in 1915? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
